---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const facts = [
  { k: "Role", v: "Product Designer · Full-time" },
  { k: "Focus", v: "Design systems, component architecture" },
  { k: "Year", v: "2026" },
];

const stats = [
  { v: "45k+", k: "providers" },
  { v: "20M+", k: "clients" },
  { v: "200+", k: "total call sites in production" },
];

const auditQuestions = [
  "Was it dismissible?",
  "How many CTAs did it have?",
  "Did it need a header?",
  "How long did the copy run, longest and shortest?",
  "Which icons were in use?",
  "Which variants were actually used, and which could be dropped?",
];

---

<CaseStudy
  title="Piloting an AI-native design system workflow from spec to production code · Cat Tran"
  description="Researching, prototyping, and shipping my team's first designer-built production component, plus the eight-month-old token bug I found along the way."
  client="Enterprise healthcare app"
  heading="Piloting an AI-native design system workflow from spec to production code"
  lede="Using an inline banner component as the pilot, I used Claude Code to conduct industry research, audit our web repo for use cases, build an app to facilitate collaborative iteration, and worked with frontend to merge and migrate into production via Storybook."
  facts={facts}
  locked
>
  <ul class="stats">
    {
      stats.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <span class="stat-v">{s.v}</span>
          <span class="stat-k">{s.k}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Context</h2>
    <p>
      I've been leading the creation and implementation of our new design
      system from the design side. I picked the inline banner as the pilot:
      small, but used everywhere, which made it a real test of owning the
      whole pipeline, from research through shipped code.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pilot made me the first non-engineer to merge code into our
      codebase, and the banners became the first components from the new
      design system to be fully migrated into our platform. As of November
      2026, I've merged 53 PRs into the design system.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Pattern research</h2>
    <p>
      Rather than start from a blank canvas, I looked at how GitHub, Atlassian,
      Adobe, and IBM handle the same pattern, sometimes called an alert,
      sometimes an inline message. I used Claude to pull their documentation,
      then had it recreate each pattern inside a real surface from our own app,
      so I could judge the options in our actual UI instead of in isolation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The responsive behavior follows Primer, GitHub's design system: at full
      width the primary action sits on the right, and in narrower containers
      the actions move left and wrap below the text.
    </p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <svg
        class="diagram"
        viewBox="0 0 680 296"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="banner-t banner-d"
      >
        <title id="banner-t">Banner anatomy across widths</title>
        <desc id="banner-d">
          A generic recreation of the banner component: an icon, optional
          header, body text, a dismiss control, and one or two CTAs. At full
          width the primary CTA aligns right; at partial width it aligns left
          and wraps below the text.
        </desc>

        <text
          x="20"
          y="22"
          font-size="11"
          font-weight="600"
          letter-spacing="0.08em"
          fill="#A8451E">FULL WIDTH</text>
        <rect
          x="20"
          y="34"
          width="640"
          height="60"
          rx="10"
          fill="#F1ECDD"
          stroke="#E6DDCC"></rect>
        <circle cx="48" cy="64" r="9" fill="none" stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.25"
        ></circle>
        <rect x="70" y="52" width="150" height="8" rx="4" fill="#6F6252"></rect>
        <rect x="70" y="66" width="240" height="7" rx="3.5" fill="#D9D0BE"
        ></rect>
        <g stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round">
          <line x1="638" y1="44" x2="646" y2="52"></line>
          <line x1="646" y1="44" x2="638" y2="52"></line>
        </g>
        <rect x="458" y="53" width="72" height="22" rx="6" fill="none"
        stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.25"></rect>
        <text x="494" y="67" font-size="10.5" fill="#2A2724" text-anchor="middle"
          >Cancel</text
        >
        <rect x="538" y="53" width="90" height="22" rx="6" fill="#A8451E"></rect>
        <text
          x="583"
          y="67"
          font-size="10.5"
          fill="#FFFFFF"
          font-weight="600"
          text-anchor="middle">Primary</text>
        <text x="20" y="112" font-size="11" fill="#6F6252">
          Primary CTA aligns right
        </text>

        <text
          x="20"
          y="150"
          font-size="11"
          font-weight="600"
          letter-spacing="0.08em"
          fill="#A8451E">PARTIAL WIDTH</text>
        <rect
          x="20"
          y="162"
          width="330"
          height="96"
          rx="10"
          fill="#F1ECDD"
          stroke="#E6DDCC"></rect>
        <circle cx="48" cy="192" r="9" fill="none" stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.25"
        ></circle>
        <rect x="70" y="180" width="140" height="8" rx="4" fill="#6F6252"></rect>
        <rect x="70" y="194" width="200" height="7" rx="3.5" fill="#D9D0BE"
        ></rect>
        <g stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round">
          <line x1="326" y1="172" x2="334" y2="180"></line>
          <line x1="334" y1="172" x2="326" y2="180"></line>
        </g>
        <rect x="70" y="220" width="84" height="22" rx="6" fill="#A8451E"></rect>
        <text
          x="112"
          y="234"
          font-size="10.5"
          fill="#FFFFFF"
          font-weight="600"
          text-anchor="middle">Primary</text>
        <rect x="162" y="220" width="72" height="22" rx="6" fill="none"
        stroke="#2A2724" stroke-width="1.25"></rect>
        <text x="198" y="234" font-size="10.5" fill="#2A2724" text-anchor="middle"
          >Cancel</text
        >
        <text x="20" y="280" font-size="11" fill="#6F6252">
          Primary CTA aligns left, CTAs wrap below the text
        </text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        A generic recreation, not the real UI. The same responsive rule
        applies to both the inline and global banners.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The sandbox</h2>
    <p>
      To workshop the direction with the team, I built an interactive
      prototype: an HTML sandbox with a dropdown for every property, corner
      radius, color, type token, number of CTAs, each wired to our real
      Tailwind tokens. The team could reconfigure the component live in design
      review instead of reacting to static comps.
    </p>
    <figure class="shot">
      <div class="sandbox-demo light-surface">
        <form class="sandbox-controls" aria-label="Banner sandbox controls">
          <div class="sandbox-field">
            <label for="sb-radius">Corner radius</label>
            <select id="sb-radius">
              <option value="4">4px</option>
              <option value="8" selected>8px</option>
              <option value="12">12px</option>
              <option value="20">20px</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div class="sandbox-field">
            <label for="sb-type">Type size</label>
            <select id="sb-type">
              <option value="14">14px</option>
              <option value="16" selected>16px</option>
              <option value="18">18px</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div class="sandbox-field">
            <label for="sb-icon-size">Icon size</label>
            <select id="sb-icon-size">
              <option value="16">16px</option>
              <option value="20" selected>20px</option>
              <option value="24">24px</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div class="sandbox-field">
            <label for="sb-fill">Fill color</label>
            <select id="sb-fill">
              <option value="#F6E4DE" selected>red-200</option>
              <option value="#EDC9BD">red-300</option>
              <option value="#FFFFFF">white</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div class="sandbox-field">
            <label for="sb-stroke">Stroke color</label>
            <select id="sb-stroke">
              <option value="#9A3B22" selected>red-600</option>
              <option value="#5C2414">red-800</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </form>

        <div class="sandbox-preview">
          <div class="sb-banner" id="sb-banner">
            <svg
              class="sb-icon"
              id="sb-icon"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              <circle
                class="sb-icon-ring"
                cx="12"
                cy="12"
                r="9"
                stroke-width="1.6"></circle>
              <rect
                class="sb-icon-mark"
                x="11"
                y="6.5"
                width="2"
                height="7"
                rx="1"></rect>
              <circle class="sb-icon-mark" cx="12" cy="16.5" r="1.2"
              ></circle>
            </svg>
            <div class="sb-body">
              <p class="sb-header-text" id="sb-header-text">Heads up</p>
              <p class="sb-copy-text" id="sb-copy-text">
                This banner is wired to the same tokens the real component
                reads.
              </p>
              <div class="sb-ctas">
                <button type="button" class="sb-btn sb-btn-primary">
                  Primary
                </button>
              </div>
            </div>
            <button
              type="button"
              class="sb-dismiss"
              aria-label="Dismiss banner"
            >
              &times;
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        A simplified rebuild of the sandbox: five dropdowns standing in for
        the real one's full token set. The banner on the right responds
        live, the same way it did in design review.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Grounding it in real usage</h2>
    <p>
      Before finalizing the API, I audited every existing instance of the
      inline banner in our web repo:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {auditQuestions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      That, plus a look at accessibility and how competitors handled the same
      cases, shaped the final properties. Nothing was a guess.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Challenge 1</h2>
    <p>
      We planned to drop the success variant for a toast pattern we barely
      used. Engineers pushed back: building toasts and migrating every success
      banner was its own project, and bundling it in risked stranding banners
      in the old pattern. We kept success for a 1:1 migration and saved toasts
      for later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Challenge 2</h2>
    <p>
      Frontend built guardrails so my PRs could ship without becoming a
      bottleneck: a Claude Skill that checked every PR against our repo's
      conventions, and a quarantine label so reviewers knew to look closer.
    </p>
    <p>
      After review, engineers ran a Chromatic audit across a large share of
      the site's banners. It surfaced a surprise: inline and global banners
      had been used interchangeably, with no rule for either. We audited the
      codebase and set one: a banner inside a container is inline, and a
      banner outside one is global. A universal find and replace then fixed
      the misuse as part of the migration, and we finished the global banner
      and shipped both together.
    </p>
    <p>
      That was safe because of an early call. The two have different usage
      guidelines, so they stayed separate components, but I designed the
      global banner to render identically to the inline one at the same
      width. Migrating a banner to the wrong one would produce a zero visual
      diff, not a flag someone overrides.
    </p>
    <p>
      I reviewed about a hundred diffs, and production still surprised us: a
      conditional banner with long copy never shows up in a diff if its
      condition isn't met.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Challenge 3</h2>
    <p>
      Meanwhile, spacing across the product kept looking slightly too big.
      With Claude Code, I traced it to a custom spacing scale overriding our
      Tailwind scale, added eight months earlier in an unreviewed PR.
      Depending on the value, it inflated spacing by 2, 4, or 12 pixels.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fixing the token outright would have shifted every component already
      on the system. Another engineer removed the custom scale as a zero-diff
      change. I followed with PRs that adjusted the tokens and put every
      button on a proper spacing ramp, based on how other systems scale
      theirs, plus a migration-only variant so components could move onto the
      new ramp with zero visual diff and get resized later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Raising it unblocked a lot of stalled work, including the CTA buttons in
      my own banners.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">What I took from it</h2>
    <p>
      Working directly in GitHub and our codebase taught me a lot about the
      engineering workflow, from reading code comments to remembering to
      rebase my PRs when they went stale.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of it would have been possible without a deeply collaborative
      frontend team. They built the Claude Skills and governance that reduced
      the risk of a non-engineer shipping code, and together we set up
      workflows for constant communication so we could respond to each other
      quickly.
    </p>
    <p>
      My biggest takeaway: how fast a team can move comes down to governance,
      communication, and shared standards.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<script>
  const radius = document.getElementById("sb-radius") as HTMLSelectElement;
  const type = document.getElementById("sb-type") as HTMLSelectElement;
  const iconSize = document.getElementById("sb-icon-size") as HTMLSelectElement;
  const fill = document.getElementById("sb-fill") as HTMLSelectElement;
  const stroke = document.getElementById("sb-stroke") as HTMLSelectElement;

  const banner = document.getElementById("sb-banner") as HTMLElement;
  const headerText = document.getElementById("sb-header-text") as HTMLElement;
  const copyText = document.getElementById("sb-copy-text") as HTMLElement;
  const icon = document.getElementById("sb-icon") as unknown as SVGElement;

  const headerLineHeight = 1.3;

  function render() {
    banner.style.borderRadius = `${radius.value}px`;

    const size = Number(type.value);
    const headerSize = size + 1;
    copyText.style.fontSize = `${size}px`;
    headerText.style.fontSize = `${headerSize}px`;

    const iconPx = Number(iconSize.value);
    icon.style.width = `${iconPx}px`;
    icon.style.height = `${iconPx}px`;
    icon.style.marginTop = `${(headerSize * headerLineHeight - iconPx) / 2}px`;

    banner.style.backgroundColor = fill.value;
    banner.style.borderColor = stroke.value;
  }

  [radius, type, iconSize, fill, stroke].forEach((el) =>
    el.addEventListener("change", render),
  );
  render();
</script>

<style>
  .sandbox-demo {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 176px 1fr;
    gap: 26px;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 12px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: #fff;
  }
  .sandbox-controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .sandbox-field {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 5px;
  }
  .sandbox-field label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--warm-grey);
  }
  .sandbox-field select {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--ink);
    padding: 7px 9px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: var(--paper);
  }
  .sandbox-preview {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 8px;
  }

  .sb-banner {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 12px;
    width: 100%;
    padding: 14px 40px 14px 16px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    background: #f1ecdd;
  }

  .sb-icon {
    flex: none;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sb-icon-ring {
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
  }
  .sb-icon-mark {
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .sb-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .sb-header-text {
    margin: 0 0 4px;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sb-copy-text {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sb-ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 10px;
  }
  .sb-btn {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    font-weight: 500;
    padding: 6px 13px;
    border-radius: 7px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sb-btn-primary {
    border: 1px solid var(--terracotta-ink);
    background: var(--terracotta-ink);
    color: #fff;
  }
  .sb-dismiss {
    position: absolute;
    top: 10px;
    right: 12px;
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    line-height: 1;
    border: none;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--warm-grey);
    font-size: 17px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sb-dismiss:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .sandbox-demo {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
